The attached image illustrates the in vivo kinetics and biodistribution of Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 nanoparticles in mice. Panel **a** shows whole-animal fluorescence imaging over 21 days, revealing that Nano-11 is primarily retained at the intramuscular injection sites (calf muscles) and gradually clears over this period. Panel **b** displays ex vivo imaging of various internal organs and injection sites at day 21, indicating minimal systemic distribution or accumulation in major organs like the heart, lung, spleen, liver, or kidneys. Panel **c** quantifies the fluorescence intensity at the injection site over time, confirming its retention and gradual clearance, with significantly reduced signals by days 14 and 21, suggesting a localized and transient presence.